-- title:
--   Soundness of $\beta\eta$ for the set-valued semantics of the simply typed lambda calculus
-- statement:
--   **Soundness of $\beta\eta$** for the set-valued semantics of the simply typed lambda calculus.
--
--   Let $\equiv_{\beta\eta}$ be the least congruence on terms of a fixed type generated by $\beta$- and $\eta$-conversion, and let $\llbracket \cdot \rrbracket_\rho$ be the denotation of a term in an environment $\rho$ under an interpretation $X$ of the base types. If $t \equiv_{\beta\eta} u$ then, for every interpretation $X$ and every environment $\rho$,
--
--   $$
--   \llbracket t \rrbracket_\rho \;=\; \llbracket u \rrbracket_\rho .
--   $$
--
--   In other words every set-valued model of the calculus validates the equational theory: equality of denotations is a congruence containing $\beta$ and $\eta$. Consequently, a proof of $t \not\equiv_{\beta\eta} u$ is a proof that $t$ and $u$ are distinguished by some set-valued model, which is the form in which the equational theory is used to refute equations between programs. The $\beta$ case of the induction is the substitution lemma and the $\eta$ case is the renaming lemma.
-- source:
--   Barendregt, The Lambda Calculus: Its Syntax and Semantics (1984), ch. 2 and 4 (soundness of $\beta\eta$ for $\lambda$-models); Lambek--Scott, Introduction to Higher Order Categorical Logic (1986), Part I, ch. 8

import Definitions.Def_STLC_core

namespace STLC

/-- **Soundness of beta-eta.** If two terms are beta-eta equivalent, then they have
the same denotation in every set-valued interpretation of the base types. -/
theorem beta_eta_sound {X : Nat → Type} :
    {Γ : List Ty} → {τ : Ty} → {t u : Tm Γ τ} → BE t u → (ρ : Env X Γ) →
      Tm.eval t ρ = Tm.eval u ρ := by
  sorry

end STLC
